    "title": "Constitutional Milestones Under the British Raj: 1773 to 1935 Acts & Indian Independence Act 1947",
    "subtitle": "Regulating Act 1773, Pitt's India Act 1784, Charter Acts, 1909 Morley-Minto, 1919 Montagu-Chelmsford & 1935 Blueprint",
    "overview": "Analyze the structural evolution of administrative and constitutional governance in India from Company rule to Crown rule.",
    "summary": "The Indian Constitution did not emerge in a historical vacuum; it inherited administrative structures, parliamentary institutions, and legal precedents developed over nearly two centuries of British rule. Key milestones include the <strong>Regulating Act of 1773</strong> (which laid the foundation of central administration and the Supreme Court at Calcutta), the <strong>Government of India Act 1858</strong> (transfer of power to the British Crown), the <strong>1909 Act</strong> (separate electorates for Muslims), the <strong>1919 Act</strong> (dyarchy in provinces), and the monumental <strong>Government of India Act 1935</strong>, which served as the structural blueprint for the 1950 Constitution.",
    "fullNotes": "<h3>1. Constitutional Evolution Under East India Company Rule (1773–1858)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Regulating Act of 1773:</strong> First step by the British Parliament to control and regulate the affairs of the East India Company. Designated the Governor of Bengal as the <strong>Governor-General of Bengal</strong> (Lord Warren Hastings). Created an Executive Council of 4 members. Provided for the establishment of a <strong>Supreme Court at Fort William, Calcutta (1774)</strong> comprising one Chief Justice (Sir Elijah Impey) and three other judges.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pitt's India Act of 1784:</strong> Distinguished commercial and political functions of the Company. Created a new <strong>Board of Control</strong> (6 commissioners) to manage political and military affairs, while the Court of Directors managed commercial affairs, establishing a <strong>'System of Double Government'</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Charter Act of 1813:</strong> Abolished the commercial monopoly of the Company in India, throwing Indian trade open to all British merchants (monopoly in tea trade and trade with China was retained). Allocated Rs 1 lakh annually for Indian education. Allowed Christian missionaries into India.</li>\n  <li><strong>Charter Act of 1833:</strong> Final step towards centralisation in British India. Made the Governor-General of Bengal the <strong>Governor-General of India</strong> (Lord William Bentinck was the first Governor-General of India). Deprived Bombay and Madras governors of legislative powers. Ended Company's commercial activities entirely (became purely administrative). Added a 4th member (Law Member: <strong>Lord Macaulay</strong>) to the Governor-General's Council.</li>\n  <li><strong>Charter Act of 1853:</strong> Separated for the first time the legislative and executive functions of the Governor-General's Council. Established a 6-member <strong>Indian (Central) Legislative Council</strong> (mini-parliament). Introduced an <strong>open competitive examination system</strong> for the selection of civil servants (Macaulay Committee on Indian Civil Service, 1854).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Constitutional Evolution Under the British Crown (1858–1947)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Government of India Act 1858 (Act for the Better Government of India):</strong> Enacted following the 1857 Revolt. Abolished the East India Company and transferred powers of government, territories, and revenues to the British Crown. Abolished the Board of Control and Court of Directors. Created the office of <strong>Secretary of State for India</strong> (member of British Cabinet, assisted by a 15-member Council of India). Designated the Governor-General as the <strong>Viceroy of India</strong> (Lord Canning was the first Viceroy).</li>\n  <li><strong>Indian Councils Act of 1861:</strong> Initiated the process of decentralisation by restoring legislative powers to Bombay and Madras. Empowered the Viceroy to issue <strong>Ordinances</strong> during emergencies (valid for 6 months). Lord Canning introduced the <strong>Portfolio System</strong>. Non-official Indians nominated to Legislative Council (Raja of Benaras, Maharaja of Patiala, Sir Dinkar Rao).</li>\n  <li><strong>Indian Councils Act of 1909 (Morley-Minto Reforms):</strong> Introduced <strong>separate electorates for Muslims</strong> (Lord Minto recognized as the 'Father of Communal Electorate'). Satyendra Prasad Sinha became the first Indian to join the Viceroy's Executive Council (appointed as Law Member).</li>\n  <li><strong>Government of India Act 1919 (Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms):</strong> Introduced <strong>Dyarchy in the provinces</strong> (dividing provincial subjects into 'Transferred' administered by ministers and 'Reserved' administered by Governor and executive council). Introduced <strong>Bicameralism</strong> and direct elections at the Centre. Created the office of the High Commissioner for India in London. Provided for the establishment of a <strong>Public Service Commission</strong> (Central PSC set up in 1926). Separated provincial budgets from the central budget.</li>\n  <li><strong>Government of India Act 1935 (The Structural Blueprint):</strong> Lengthiest British statute (321 sections, 10 schedules). Provided for an <strong>All-India Federation</strong> (consisting of provinces and princely states, which never came into being). Divided powers into three lists: <strong>Federal List (59 items), Provincial List (54 items), and Concurrent List (36 items)</strong>; residuary powers vested in the Viceroy. <strong>Abolished Dyarchy in provinces and introduced Provincial Autonomy</strong>. Introduced Dyarchy at the Centre (never implemented). Established the <strong>Federal Court of India (1937)</strong>, the <strong>Reserve Bank of India (1935)</strong>, and Federal/Provincial Public Service Commissions. Approximately 70% of the administrative machinery of the Indian Constitution is drawn from this Act.</li>\n  <li><strong>Indian Independence Act 1947:</strong> Enacted on July 18, 1947 (Mountbatten Plan of June 3, 1947). Declared India as an independent and sovereign state from August 15, 1947. Created two independent Dominions: India and Pakistan. Abolished the office of Viceroy and Secretary of State. Empowered Constituent Assemblies of both dominions to legislate and draft their constitutions.</li>\n</ul>",
