    "diagram": "diag-pol-law-making-process.svg",
    "topicId": "top-pol-02-02",
    "subjectId": "gk-polity",
    "introduction": "Master the unique structural blend of rigidity, flexibility, judicial supremacy, and parliamentary sovereignty in India.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Unique Structural Attributes of the Indian Constitution</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Lengthiest Written Constitution in the World:</strong> Originally had 395 Articles in 22 Parts and 8 Schedules. Currently contains over 470 Articles in 25 Parts and 12 Schedules. Comprehensive size is attributed to geographical diversity, historical influence of the 1935 Act, single constitution for both Centre and States, and dominance of legal luminaries in the Assembly.</li>\n  <li><strong>Drawn from Diverse Global Sources:</strong> Synthesized best practices across American, British, Irish, Canadian, Australian, French, and Soviet models.</li>\n  <li><strong>Blend of Rigidity and Flexibility:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Some provisions can be amended by a <strong>Simple Majority</strong> of Parliament (e.g., creation of new states under Art 3, abolition of legislative councils under Art 169) &ndash; Flexible.</li>\n      <li>Most provisions require a <strong>Special Majority</strong> of Parliament (majority of total membership + 2/3rd members present and voting under Art 368) &ndash; Moderately Rigid.</li>\n      <li>Federal provisions require a <strong>Special Majority of Parliament PLUS Ratification by at least half of State Legislatures</strong> (e.g., election of President, Supreme Court powers, 7th Schedule lists) &ndash; Rigid.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Federal System with Unitary Bias (Quasi-Federal):</strong> Possesses typical federal features (two governments, division of powers, written constitution, supremacy of constitution, bicameralism, independent judiciary) alongside powerful unitary features (single citizenship, integrated judiciary, emergency powers, appointment of Governor by Centre, All-India Services, CAG, single Election Commission).</li>\n  <li><strong>Synthesis of Parliamentary Sovereignty & Judicial Supremacy:</strong> While Parliament can amend the Constitution, the Supreme Court can invalidate parliamentary laws under Judicial Review (Arts 13, 32, 136) if they breach Fundamental Rights or the Basic Structure.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Democratic & Institutional Guarantees</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Integrated and Independent Judiciary:</strong> A single integrated judicial hierarchy enforces both Central and State laws (unlike the US dual-court system). Supreme Court at the apex, High Courts at state level, Subordinate Courts at district level.</li>\n  <li><strong>Universal Adult Suffrage (Article 326):</strong> Every citizen aged 18 years and above has the right to vote without discrimination. The voting age was reduced from <strong>21 years to 18 years by the 61st Constitutional Amendment Act, 1988</strong> (effective March 1989).</li>\n  <li><strong>Single Citizenship:</strong> Every citizen possesses only one single Indian citizenship, fostering national unity (unlike the dual federal/state citizenship of the US and Switzerland).</li>\n  <li><strong>Independent Constitutional Bodies:</strong> Bulwarks of democratic governance: Election Commission of India (Art 324), Comptroller and Auditor General of India (Art 148), UPSC and State PSCs (Arts 315–323).</li>\n  <li><strong>Three-Tier Governance:</strong> Originally two-tier (Centre and States); 73rd and 74th Amendments (1992) added a constitutional third tier of local self-government (Panchayats and Municipalities).</li>\n</ul>",
