    "introduction": "Examine the socio-economic welfare blueprint of Part IV, classification of DPSPs, and their constitutional balance with Fundamental Rights.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Classification of Directive Principles of State Policy</h3>\n<p>Although the Constitution does not classify DPSPs, scholars categorize them into three broad philosophical groups:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>1. Socialistic Principles (Reflecting Democratic Socialism & Economic Welfare):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Article 38:</em> Promote the welfare of the people by securing a social order permeated by social, economic, and political justice, and minimizing inequalities in income, status, facilities, and opportunities (Clause 2 added by 44th Amendment, 1978).</li>\n      <li><em>Article 39:</em> Secure: (a) adequate means of livelihood, (b) equitable distribution of material resources of the community for common good, (c) prevention of concentration of wealth and means of production, (d) <strong>equal pay for equal work for men and women</strong>, (e) protection of health and strength of workers, and (f) healthy development of children (amended by 42nd Amendment).</li>\n      <li><em>Article 39A:</em> <strong>Equal justice and free legal aid</strong> to the poor (inserted by 42nd Amendment, 1976; statutory basis for NALSA and Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987).</li>\n      <li><em>Article 41:</em> Right to work, to education, and to public assistance in cases of unemployment, old age, sickness, and disablement (ideological inspiration for MGNREGA).</li>\n      <li><em>Article 42:</em> Provision for just and humane conditions of work and <strong>maternity relief</strong>.</li>\n      <li><em>Article 43:</em> Secure a living wage, decent standard of life, and social/cultural opportunities for all workers.</li>\n      <li><em>Article 43A:</em> <strong>Participation of workers in management of industries</strong> (inserted by 42nd Amendment, 1976).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Gandhian Principles (Embodying Mahatma Gandhi's Reconstruction Program):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Article 40:</em> <strong>Organisation of Village Panchayats</strong> and endowing them with powers to function as units of self-government (realized by the 73rd Amendment, 1992).</li>\n      <li><em>Article 43:</em> Promotion of cottage industries on an individual or cooperative basis in rural areas.</li>\n      <li><em>Article 43B:</em> Promotion of voluntary formation and autonomous functioning of <strong>cooperative societies</strong> (inserted by <strong>97th Constitutional Amendment Act, 2011</strong>).</li>\n      <li><em>Article 46:</em> Promotion of educational and economic interests of SCs, STs, and other weaker sections, protecting them from social injustice.</li>\n      <li><em>Article 47:</em> <strong>Prohibition of the consumption of intoxicating drinks and drugs</strong> injurious to health (constitutional basis for liquor prohibition policies).</li>\n      <li><em>Article 48:</em> <strong>Prohibition of the slaughter of cows, calves, and other milch and draught cattle</strong>, and organizing agriculture and animal husbandry on modern scientific lines.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>3. Liberal-Intellectual Principles (Ideology of Liberalism & Rule of Law):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Article 44:</em> <strong>Uniform Civil Code (UCC)</strong> for all citizens throughout the territory of India. Goa was historically the only state with a Portuguese-era common civil code; Uttarakhand enacted India's first post-independence UCC legislation in 2024.</li>\n      <li><em>Article 45:</em> Early childhood care and education for all children until they complete the age of six years (amended by 86th Amendment, 2002 after Art 21A was enacted).</li>\n      <li><em>Article 48A:</em> <strong>Protection and improvement of environment and safeguarding of forests and wild life</strong> (inserted by 42nd Amendment, 1976).</li>\n      <li><em>Article 49:</em> Protection of monuments, places, and objects of artistic or historic interest declared to be of national importance.</li>\n      <li><em>Article 50:</em> <strong>Separation of the Judiciary from the Executive</strong> in the public services of the State.</li>\n      <li><em>Article 51:</em> Promotion of <strong>international peace and security</strong>, maintaining just and honorable relations between nations, and fostering respect for international law and treaty obligations.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. DPSP vs Fundamental Rights: Constitutional Harmonisation</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Champakam Dorairajan Case (1951):</strong> Supreme Court held that in case of conflict between Fundamental Rights and DPSPs, <strong>Fundamental Rights prevail</strong>; DPSPs run as subsidiary to Fundamental Rights.</li>\n  <li><strong>Golaknath Case (1967):</strong> Supreme Court held that Fundamental Rights are transcendental and sacrosanct and cannot be amended to give effect to DPSPs.</li>\n  <li><strong>25th Amendment Act, 1971:</strong> Inserted <strong>Article 31C</strong>, providing that laws giving effect to DPSPs in Article 39(b) and 39(c) shall not be void on grounds of violating Articles 14, 19, or 31.</li>\n  <li><strong>Minerva Mills Case (1980):</strong> Supreme Court struck down the 42nd Amendment's extension of Art 31C and established that the Indian Constitution is founded on the <strong>bedrock of the balance between Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles</strong>. To give primacy to one over the other is to upend the harmony of the Constitution.</li>\n</ul>",
