    "diagram": "diag-pol-law-making-process.svg",
    "topicId": "top-pol-05-01",
    "subjectId": "gk-polity",
    "introduction": "Master the constitutional status, election arithmetic, tenure, impeachment procedure, and executive prerogatives of the President.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Election of the President (Articles 54 & 55)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Electoral College (Article 54):</strong> The President is elected NOT directly by the people, but by an Electoral College consisting of:\n    <ol>\n      <li><strong>Elected members of both Houses of Parliament (MPs)</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Elected members of the Legislative Assemblies of the States (MLAs)</strong>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Elected members of the Legislative Assemblies of the Union Territories of Delhi and Puducherry</strong> (added by the <strong>70th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992</strong>), and Jammu & Kashmir.</li>\n      <li><em>NOT in Electoral College:</em> Nominated members of Parliament, nominated members of State Assemblies, and <strong>ALL members of State Legislative Councils (MLCs)</strong>.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Manner of Election (Article 55):</strong> System of proportional representation by means of the single transferable vote and voting by secret ballot. Uniformity in the scale of representation of different states is ensured by mathematical formulas:\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Value of Vote of an MLA:</em> [Total Population of State (1971 Census)] / [Total Elected MLAs of State &times; 1,000]. (Uttar Pradesh has the highest MLA vote value in India: <strong>208</strong>; Sikkim has the lowest: <strong>7</strong>).</li>\n      <li><em>Value of Vote of an MP:</em> [Total Value of Votes of all MLAs of all States] / [Total Number of Elected MPs of both Houses].</li>\n      <li><em>Electoral Quota:</em> [Total Valid Votes Polled / (1 + 1)] + 1 = [Total Valid Votes / 2] + 1.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Qualifications (Article 58):</strong> Citizen of India, completed <strong>35 years of age</strong>, qualified for election as a member of the Lok Sabha, must not hold any office of profit. Nomination must be subscribed by at least <strong>50 proposers and 50 seconders</strong> (security deposit Rs 15,000 with RBI).</li>\n  <li><strong>Disputes (Article 71):</strong> All doubts and disputes arising out of the election of the President or Vice-President are inquired into and decided <strong>exclusively by the Supreme Court of India</strong>, whose decision is final.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Term, Vacancy & Impeachment (Articles 56, 61, 62)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Term of Office:</strong> 5 years from the date of entering office. Eligible for re-election for any number of terms (Dr. Rajendra Prasad was the only President elected twice).</li>\n  <li><strong>Resignation:</strong> Addressed in writing to the <strong>Vice-President of India</strong> (who immediately communicates it to the Speaker of the Lok Sabha).</li>\n  <li><strong>Impeachment Procedure (Article 61):</strong> The President can be removed from office ONLY for <strong>'Violation of the Constitution'</strong> (a phrase not defined in the Constitution).\n    <ol>\n      <li>Impeachment charges can be initiated in <strong>either House of Parliament</strong>.</li>\n      <li>Resolution must be signed by at least <strong>1/4th of the total members</strong> of that House and a <strong>14-day advance notice</strong> must be given to the President.</li>\n      <li>The resolution must be passed by a <strong>majority of not less than two-thirds of the TOTAL membership</strong> of that House.</li>\n      <li>The other House investigates the charges; the President has the right to appear and be represented.</li>\n      <li>If the investigating House also passes the resolution by a <strong>majority of not less than two-thirds of its TOTAL membership</strong>, the President stands removed from office. (No President of India has ever been impeached).</li>\n    </ol>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Discharge of Functions (Presidential Succession):</strong> If the office falls vacant due to death, resignation, or removal, the <strong>Vice-President</strong> acts as President. If the Vice-President is unavailable, the <strong>Chief Justice of India</strong> acts as President (Justice M. Hidayatullah acted as President in 1969). Election to fill vacancy must be held within <strong>6 months</strong>.</li>\n</ul>",
