    "introduction": "Master the dual role of the Vice-President, the Cabinet system of governance, and the constitutional position of the Prime Minister.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. The Vice-President of India (Articles 63 to 70)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Constitutional Office:</strong> Modeled on the American Vice-President. Functions as the <strong>ex-officio Chairman of the Council of States (Rajya Sabha)</strong> under Article 64. Draws his salary and emoluments in his capacity as Rajya Sabha Chairman (not as Vice-President).</li>\n  <li><strong>Electoral College (Article 66):</strong> Elected by members of an Electoral College consisting of:\n    <ul>\n      <li><strong>Members of both Houses of Parliament (both elected AND nominated members)</strong>.</li>\n      <li><em>Distinction from Presidential Election:</em> Does NOT include members of State Legislative Assemblies (MLAs). Includes nominated members of Parliament.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Qualifications:</strong> Citizen of India, minimum <strong>35 years of age</strong>, qualified for election as a member of the <strong>Rajya Sabha</strong>, must not hold an office of profit. Proposers and seconders: <strong>20 proposers and 20 seconders</strong> (security deposit Rs 15,000).</li>\n  <li><strong>Removal Procedure (Article 67(b)):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Resolution can be initiated <strong>ONLY in the Rajya Sabha</strong>.</li>\n      <li>Requires a <strong>14-day advance notice</strong>.</li>\n      <li>Must be passed by the Rajya Sabha by an <strong>Effective Majority</strong> (majority of all the then members) and <strong>agreed to by the Lok Sabha</strong> by a Simple Majority. (No formal impeachment like the President).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Casting Vote (Article 100):</strong> As Chairman of the Rajya Sabha, the Vice-President does not vote in the first instance, but exercises a <strong>casting vote</strong> in case of an equality of votes (tie).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Prime Minister & The Union Council of Ministers (Articles 74, 75, 78)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>De Jure vs De Facto Executive:</strong> The President is the nominal, constitutional executive (<em>de jure</em>), while the Prime Minister is the real executive (<em>de facto</em>) and head of government.</li>\n  <li><strong>Article 74:</strong> <em>'There shall be a Council of Ministers with the Prime Minister at the head to aid and advise the President...'</em>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>42nd Amendment Act, 1976:</em> Made the advice of the Council of Ministers <strong>binding on the President</strong>.</li>\n      <li><em>44th Amendment Act, 1978:</em> Added a proviso empowering the President to require the Council of Ministers to <strong>reconsider such advice once</strong>; however, the President is bound to act in accordance with the advice tendered after reconsideration.</li>\n      <li>Article 74(2) bars courts from inquiring into what advice was tendered by Ministers to the President.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Appointment & Size of Council (Article 75 & 91st Amendment):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Prime Minister is appointed by the President; other ministers appointed by President on advice of the Prime Minister.</li>\n      <li><strong>91st Constitutional Amendment Act, 2003:</strong> Added Article 75(1A), stipulating that the total number of ministers, including the Prime Minister, in the Union Council of Ministers <strong>shall not exceed 15% of the total number of members of the Lok Sabha</strong>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Collective Responsibility (Article 75(3)):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>The Council of Ministers is <strong>collectively responsible to the House of the People (Lok Sabha)</strong>. When a No-Confidence Motion is passed in the Lok Sabha, the entire ministry (including ministers from Rajya Sabha) must resign ('they swim or sink together').</li>\n      <li>Individual Responsibility (Art 75(2)): Ministers hold office during the pleasure of the President (exercised on advice of the PM).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Article 78 (Duties of Prime Minister):</strong> Channel of communication between the President and the Council of Ministers; duty of PM to communicate all decisions of the Council to the President.</li>\n</ul>",
