    "introduction": "Master the parliamentary passage of Ordinary, Money, and Financial Bills, deadlocks, and presidential vetoes.",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Money Bills (Article 110) & Special Procedure (Article 109)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Definition of Money Bill (Article 110):</strong> A Bill is deemed to be a Money Bill if it contains ONLY provisions dealing with:\n    <ul>\n      <li>(a) The imposition, abolition, remission, alteration, or regulation of any tax.</li>\n      <li>(b) Regulation of borrowing of money or giving of guarantees by the Government of India.</li>\n      <li>(c) Custody of the Consolidated Fund or Contingency Fund of India, payment of money into or withdrawal of money from such Fund.</li>\n      <li>(d) Appropriation of moneys out of the Consolidated Fund of India.</li>\n      <li>(e) Declaring any expenditure to be expenditure charged on the Consolidated Fund of India.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Exclusive Speaker Certification:</strong> Under Article 110(3), if any question arises whether a Bill is a Money Bill or not, the <strong>decision of the Speaker of the Lok Sabha is final</strong>. The Speaker signs a certificate endorsing it as a Money Bill before transmitting it to the Rajya Sabha and the President.</li>\n  <li><strong>Special Procedure for Money Bills (Article 109):</strong>\n    <ol>\n      <li>A Money Bill can be introduced <strong>ONLY in the Lok Sabha</strong>.</li>\n      <li>Can be introduced ONLY on the prior recommendation of the <strong>President</strong>.</li>\n      <li>Cannot be introduced by a private member (must be a Government Bill).</li>\n      <li>The Rajya Sabha has strictly limited powers: It <strong>cannot reject or amend</strong> a Money Bill; it can only make recommendations.</li>\n      <li>The Rajya Sabha must return the Bill within <strong>14 days</strong> with or without recommendations. If not returned within 14 days, the Bill is deemed to have been passed by both Houses.</li>\n      <li>Lok Sabha may accept or reject any of Rajya Sabha's recommendations.</li>\n      <li>There is <strong>NO provision for a Joint Sitting on a Money Bill</strong>.</li>\n      <li>The President must either give assent or withhold assent; <strong>CANNOT return a Money Bill for reconsideration</strong>.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Ordinary Bills & Joint Sitting of Parliament (Article 108)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Ordinary Bills:</strong> Can originate in either House; must be passed by both Houses by a simple majority of members present and voting. Rajya Sabha can hold an Ordinary Bill for a maximum period of <strong>6 months</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Joint Sitting (Article 108):</strong> Summoned by the President if an Ordinary Bill is passed by one House and:\n    <ol>\n      <li>The other House rejects the Bill; or</li>\n      <li>The Houses have finally disagreed as to amendments; or</li>\n      <li>More than <strong>6 months elapse</strong> without the Bill being passed by the other House.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Conduct & Rules of Joint Sitting:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Presided over by the <strong>Speaker of the Lok Sabha</strong> (if absent, Deputy Speaker; if absent, Deputy Chairman of Rajya Sabha; <em>Chairman of Rajya Sabha NEVER presides</em> over a Joint Sitting).</li>\n      <li>Governed by Lok Sabha rules of procedure.</li>\n      <li>Bill is passed by a <strong>Simple Majority of the total members of both Houses present and voting</strong>. (Due to numerical superiority, Lok Sabha's will invariably prevails).</li>\n      <li><em>Joint Sitting is NOT permissible for:</em> (1) <strong>Money Bills</strong> (Lok Sabha supreme), and (2) <strong>Constitutional Amendment Bills under Article 368</strong> (must be passed by each House separately by special majority).</li>\n      <li><em>Historical Joint Sittings:</em> Held only thrice in Indian history: (1) Dowry Prohibition Bill, 1961; (2) Banking Service Commission (Repeal) Bill, 1978; (3) Prevention of Terrorism Bill (POTA), 2002.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Annual Financial Statement (The Budget – Article 112)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>The term 'Budget' is not used in the Constitution; Article 112 refers to it as the <strong>'Annual Financial Statement'</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Distinguishes between <strong>Expenditure Charged upon the Consolidated Fund of India</strong> (non-votable; discussed by Parliament but not voted upon, e.g., salaries of President, Judges of SC/HC, CAG) and <strong>Expenditure Made from the Consolidated Fund</strong> (votable as Demands for Grants in Lok Sabha).</li>\n  <li><strong>Appropriation Bill (Article 114):</strong> Authorizes withdrawal of funds from the Consolidated Fund of India; no money can be withdrawn without parliamentary enactment of an Appropriation Act.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cut Motions:</strong> Disapproval of Policy Cut (reduced to Re 1), Economy Cut (reduced by specified amount), Token Cut (reduced by Rs 100). Passing of a Cut Motion in Lok Sabha reflects loss of majority and leads to government resignation.</li>\n</ul>",
