    "title": "Parliamentary Committees: Public Accounts Committee (PAC), Estimates Committee & Committee on Public Undertakings",
    "subtitle": "Standing Financial Committees, CAG Report Scrutiny, PAC 22 Members (15 LS + 7 RS), Estimates 30 Members & Departmental Standing Committees",
    "overview": "Master the composition, functions, and oversight mechanisms of Parliament's standing financial and departmental committees.",
    "summary": "Given the enormous volume of legislative and financial business, Parliament relies on specialized cross-party <strong>Parliamentary Committees</strong> to conduct detailed scrutiny, demand accountability, and audit government expenditures. The three premier <strong>Standing Financial Committees</strong> are the <strong>Public Accounts Committee (PAC)</strong>, the <strong>Estimates Committee</strong>, and the <strong>Committee on Public Undertakings (COPU)</strong>, complemented by 24 Departmentally Related Standing Committees (DRSCs).",
    "fullNotes": "<h3>1. The Three Standing Financial Committees</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>1. Public Accounts Committee (PAC):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Origin & Composition:</em> First set up in <strong>1921</strong> under the Government of India Act, 1919. Consists of <strong>22 members</strong> (<strong>15 from Lok Sabha and 7 from Rajya Sabha</strong>) elected annually by proportional representation by single transferable vote. A Minister CANNOT be elected as a member.</li>\n      <li><em>Chairman Convention:</em> Since 1967, by unbroken parliamentary convention, the Chairman of the PAC is appointed by the Speaker from the <strong>Opposition</strong>.</li>\n      <li><em>Functions:</em> Examines the annual audit reports of the <strong>Comptroller and Auditor General of India (CAG)</strong> (Appropriation Accounts and Finance Accounts) submitted to the President under Article 151. Serves as the guardian of the public purse, ensuring money was legally available and spent for the approved purpose. The CAG acts as the <strong>'Friend, Philosopher, and Guide'</strong> of the PAC.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>2. Estimates Committee:</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Origin & Composition:</em> First set up in <strong>1950</strong> on the recommendation of Finance Minister <strong>John Mathai</strong>. The <strong>largest committee of Parliament</strong>, consisting of <strong>30 members, ALL FROM LOK SABHA</strong>. (The Rajya Sabha has NO representation in the Estimates Committee). Members elected annually by proportional representation; Ministers cannot be members.</li>\n      <li><em>Chairman:</em> Appointed by the Speaker, invariably from the ruling party.</li>\n      <li><em>Functions:</em> Termed the 'Continuous Economy Committee'; examines the budget estimates to suggest alternative policies, economies in expenditure, and administrative efficiency. (It examines estimates only AFTER they have been voted upon).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>3. Committee on Public Undertakings (COPU):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Origin & Composition:</em> Created in <strong>1964</strong> on the recommendation of the <strong>Krishna Menon Committee</strong>. Consists of <strong>22 members</strong> (<strong>15 from Lok Sabha and 7 from Rajya Sabha</strong>). Members elected annually; Ministers excluded.</li>\n      <li><em>Functions:</em> Examines the reports and accounts of Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs) and CAG audit reports on public enterprises to ensure sound commercial business principles.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Other Prominent Parliamentary Committees</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Departmentally Related Standing Committees (DRSCs):</strong> 24 committees (16 under Lok Sabha, 8 under Rajya Sabha). Each consists of <strong>31 members</strong> (21 from Lok Sabha and 10 from Rajya Sabha). They scrutinize Demands for Grants of concerned ministries during budget recess.</li>\n  <li><strong>Committee on Petitions & Privileges:</strong> Inquires into breaches of parliamentary privileges under Article 105.</li>\n  <li><strong>Business Advisory Committee (BAC):</strong> Chaired ex-officio by the Speaker in Lok Sabha and Chairman in Rajya Sabha; allocates time for legislative debates.</li>\n  <li><strong>Joint Parliamentary Committee (JPC):</strong> Ad-hoc investigative committee set up for a specific inquiry (e.g., Bofors, 2G spectrum, financial scams) with proportional representation from both Houses.</li>\n</ul>",
